# ============================================================================
#  TEMPLATE for the server's secret file. Copy to /etc/ejentic-rag/server.env
#  ON THE SERVER, fill in real values there, chmod 0600, and never commit it.
#
#  Variable NAMES only — no value in this file is real, and none ever should be.
#  It is committed; the file it becomes is not. That is the same split the client
#  config uses (backend/clients/<id>.json names env vars, never holds secrets).
#
#      sudo mkdir -p /etc/ejentic-rag
#      sudo cp deploy/server.env.example /etc/ejentic-rag/server.env
#      sudo chmod 600 /etc/ejentic-rag/server.env
#      sudo chown root:root /etc/ejentic-rag/server.env
#      sudo -e /etc/ejentic-rag/server.env      # fill in the values
#
#  ⚠️ GENERATE FRESH KEYS FOR THE SERVER. Do not copy the ones from your local
#  backend/.env. A development key that has sat in a laptop's dotfile, been echoed
#  into a terminal, and appeared in shell history is not a production credential.
#  If the local keys ever leak, a deployment sharing them leaks with them.
# ============================================================================

# --- Which tenant this instance serves --------------------------------------
# Must match a file in backend/clients/ (e.g. "ejentic" -> clients/ejentic.json).
RAG_CLIENT=

# --- Upstream provider keys --------------------------------------------------
PINECONE_API_KEY=
NVIDIA_API_KEY=

# --- Clearance keys: THE SECURITY BOUNDARY -----------------------------------
# One per role. The server reads a caller's clearance FROM THE KEY, so a request
# can no longer just declare "clearance_level": "executive" and read board-only
# material. Generate three DIFFERENT values:
#
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#
# With the client config's auth.required = true (it is), the backend REFUSES TO
# BOOT if these are missing. That looks like a broken deploy the first time it
# happens; it is the fail-closed design working. Check `docker compose logs
# backend` before assuming anything else is wrong.
RAG_KEY_GUEST=
RAG_KEY_EMPLOYEE=
RAG_KEY_EXECUTIVE=

# --- DECISION 2: which key the deployed UI holds -----------------------------
# One deployed UI holds ONE key and therefore answers at ONE clearance for
# everyone who opens it. There is no "log in and see more" without real per-user
# login, which does not exist yet.
#
# Set this to the VALUE of one of the three above — normally the guest key.
# Choosing the employee or executive key here means every visitor who reaches the
# page reads at that tier, password gate or not.
#
# If staff genuinely need the internal tier, the honest options are:
#   (a) a SECOND deployment on a private hostname whose RAG_UI_KEY is the employee
#       key (cheap: another compose project + another Caddy block), or
#   (b) real user login in the frontend (a much bigger piece of work).
# Do not solve it by handing everyone the employee key.
RAG_UI_KEY=

# --- LLM provider swap (needed since the NVIDIA hosted LLM degraded) ---------
# The client config now names a Gemini model, which only works against the
# OpenAI-compatible Gemini endpoint. Without these, the LLM falls back to the
# NVIDIA stack and 404s on a model name it does not serve. LLM_API_KEY mirrors
# GOOGLE_API_KEY — see main.py LLM_SWAP_KWARGS.
LLM_BASE_URL=
LLM_API_KEY=

# --- Browser origins allowed to call the backend directly -------------------
# The real HTTPS origin, e.g. https://rag.example.com. Comma-separated for more
# than one. The backend sets allow_credentials=True, so this must never be "*":
# that combination lets any web page on the internet read this instance out of a
# visitor's browser session. Compose refuses to start if this is unset.
RAG_CORS_ORIGINS=

# --- Optional overrides ------------------------------------------------------
# Where the compose file reads the secrets from. Leave unset on the server; it
# exists only so the compose file can be validated without root.
# RAG_SERVER_ENV=

# Seconds per LLM request. NVIDIA calls can be slow; the Caddy block allows 330s,
# so keep this under that or the proxy gives up first.
# LLM_TIMEOUT=300

# Per-tenant audit DB. Defaults to /app/data/<RAG_CLIENT>/audit.db inside the
# container, which is on the rag_data named volume and therefore survives
# `docker compose down`. Override only for an external volume.
# RAG_AUDIT_DB=

# --- Business metrics (optional, OFF by default) ------------------------------
# Reports per-query usage + AI cost to the agency metrics hub, so this client's
# contribution to revenue and margin can be measured. Unset = nothing emitted,
# which is the correct setting for most servers.
#
# ⚠️ CONTAINER PATH, AND THE HUB IS NOT IN THIS IMAGE. The hub is a separate
# private repo that normally lives on a workstation, so setting this on the
# server also requires mounting it into the container (a volume in
# docker-compose.prod.yml) and pointing this at the path INSIDE the container.
# Setting it to a host path alone does nothing — the container cannot see it.
# Until that mount exists, production spend is recorded in this instance's own
# audit DB (GET /metrics) and has to be carried across by hand.
# METRICS_HUB=/app/metrics
# METRICS_SYSTEM_ID=rag

# --- Cost ceilings (optional; unset = the tenant config's values) -------------
# THE KNOB YOU WILL REACH FOR IN A HURRY. Limits are per authenticated ROLE and
# live in clients/<id>.json, but a limit set too tight is indistinguishable from
# an outage to the people hitting it, so both windows can be changed here and
# picked up with a plain `systemctl restart` — no rebuild, no editing files
# inside the deployed copy.
#
# ⚠️ ONE BUCKET PER ROLE, NOT PER PERSON. The public UI holds ONE guest key, so
# every visitor shares the guest bucket: MAX_REQUESTS_PER_DAY is the whole
# public site's daily budget, not one visitor's. Size it against expected TOTAL
# traffic. At the shipped 500 it takes ~50 visitors asking ~10 questions each to
# exhaust the day, after which every visitor sees 429 until the window rolls.
#
# The ceiling these buy you, worst case:
#   MAX_REQUESTS_PER_DAY x max_output_tokens = completion tokens/day, per role
#   500 x 2048 = 1,024,000
# On a free-tier or metered provider, set this BELOW the provider's own quota so
# callers get our 429 + Retry-After rather than the provider's raw 429 mid-demo.
#
# Unset or blank = use the config (limits stay ON — absence never means "no
# limit"). `off` disables a window and logs a warning at boot. `0` CLOSES it.
# MAX_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=30
# MAX_REQUESTS_PER_DAY=500
